{"name":"Bats Cloak","hex":"#1f1518","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bats","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of bat"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of bat"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bats"},{"term":"cloak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long outer garment worn over the shoulders covering the back; a cape, often with a hood.","A blanket-like covering, often metaphorical."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover as with or like a cloak.","To cover up, hide or conceal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cloak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bat","blocks":[{"text":"Bats are winged mammals, the only mammals capable of true and sustained flight. Bats are more agile in flight than most birds, using long, spread-out digits covered with a thin membrane or patagium. The smallest bat, and one of the smallest extant mammals, is Kitti's hog-nosed bat, which is 29–33 mm (1.1–1.3 in) in length, 150 mm (5.9 in) across the forearm and 2 g (0.071 oz) in mass. The largest bats are the flying foxes, with the giant golden-crowned flying fox reaching a weight of 1.5 kg (3.3 lb) and having a wingspan of 1.6 m."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Big-eared-townsend-fledermaus.jpg/330px-Big-eared-townsend-fledermaus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bat"},{"title":"Cloak","blocks":[{"text":"A cloak is a type of loose garment worn over clothing, mostly but not always, as outerwear for outdoor wear, which serves the same purpose as an overcoat and protects the wearer from the weather. It may form part of a uniform. People in many different societies may wear cloaks. Over time, cloak designs have changed to match fashion and available textiles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Fashion_Plate_Manteau_1823.jpg/330px-Fashion_Plate_Manteau_1823.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloak"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Bat_range.png/500px-Bat_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Bat_range.png/1280px-Bat_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Bat_range.png/500px-Bat_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABat_range.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Batfossil.jpg/330px-Batfossil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Batfossil.jpg/330px-Batfossil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The early Eocene fossil microchiropteran Icaronycteris, from the Green River Formation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABatfossil.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Zalophus_californianus_J._Smit_%28white_background%29.jpg/500px-Zalophus_californianus_J._Smit_%28white_background%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Zalophus_californianus_J._Smit_%28white_background%29.jpg/500px-Zalophus_californianus_J._Smit_%28white_background%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZalophus_californianus_J._Smit_(white_background).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Equus_quagga_%28white_background%29.jpg/500px-Equus_quagga_